$info=esb,
$bio

The Empire Strikes Back (c) 03/1985 Atari Games.


The third arcade outing based on the legendary trilogy of films; Empire Strikes Back eschews Jedi's not entirely succesful "Zaxxon"-inspired gameplay, and returns once more to the colour vector graphics of the first game. The game puts the player in control of both a snow speeder and the Millenium Falcon and features four different play areas.


* The first level, Probots, takes place on the ice planet of Hoth, and the player must find and destroy the Empire's search droids that are sending signals to the Empire warning them of the player's presence.


* The second stage sees the player taking on the Empire's 'AT-AT' Imperial walkers, as well as a number of smaller, bi-pedal walkers. On this level, the player is armed with a laser as well as a limited number of harpoons. The latter can be fired at the AT-AT's legs to send them crashing to the ground. AT-ATs can also be destroyed by shooting a small, highlighted area on the AT-ATs' 'head'.


* The third level is a repeat of the original game's 'shoot the tie-fighters' level and again takes place in outer space.


* The forth level takes place in an asteroid field and the player must carefully avoid the oncomming asteroids.


Despite being based on the SECOND movie of the series, Empire game was actually the third game of the series, with "Return of the Jedi" being released a year earlier.


- TECHNICAL -


Game ID : 136031


Main CPU : M6809 (@ 1.5 Mhz)

Sound CPU : M6809 (@ 1.5 Mhz)

Sound Chips : (4x) POKEY (@ 1.5 Mhz), TMS5220 (@ 640 Khz)


Screen orientation : Horizontal

Video resolution : 256 x 231 pixels

Aspect Ratio : 4/3

Screen refresh : 30.00 Hz

Palette Colors : 32768


Players : 1

Control : stick

Buttons : 4


This title was sold only as a conversion kit for the original "Star Wars". Separate kits were available for both the upright and the cockpit versions. The game itself is displayed on an Amplifone colour vector open frame monitor. The cabinet itself is desgined to have a Darth Vader-esque feel. Its colour scheme is very dark and is covered with lines resembling the surface of an Imperial Star Destroyer. The sideart features a picture that includes Darth Vader, the Death Star, several Tie fighters and an X-Wing. Most machines will have a sticker placed somewhere on the side labeling them as "The Empire Strikes Back". The marquee shows a painted scene of a battle on Hoth that includes several AT-AT walkers and advertises that the game is 'New'.


- TRIVIA -


'AT-AT' is an abbreviation of 'All Terrain Attack Transports'.


David Palmer holds the official record for this game with 1,345,049 points.


- SERIES -


1. Star Wars (1983)

2. Return of The Jedi (1984)

3. The Empire Strikes Back (1985)


